No, it's awful because it artificially over-constrains design.

With the scroll of genocide in the game, you cannot make interesting
or challenging monsters beyond a certain point unless they are also 
non-genocidable -- because people simply genocide them.  

Making too many things non-genocidable goes down the classic RPG failure 
road, where the player learns "Sleep" and then discovers that it 
mysteriously doesn't work against anything he might actually need to use it on.

If you take the route of making so many 'hard' monsters that the player
doesn't have enough scrolls of genocide, then you create a different
problem, one more appropriate to Slash'EM.

>> Yes.  They're big, evil monsters.  You're supposed to be wary of them,
>> not brush them off like a piece of dandruff.  (cf. vanilla)
>
>> You are significantly exaggerating the situation.
>
> If you put the two statements above together, you'll see I'm not the
