Actually, the above statement is a flat truth in vanilla.

I genocide L in vanilla as a pure time-saver; the one thing they can do
that impedes my progress is create a summon storm.  The geno isn't because
the summon storm might kill me; it's because it will stop me from
moving, which will cost me time while I carve a path out with Elbereth
and judicious chopping.

Cursing?  Elbereth.  Everything else?  Covered by MR.  If they didn't
teleport in front of me constantly and block my path, I'd probably not
even bother killing them.  They mean _that little_ in vanilla once you
have MR.  The arch-liches even drop athames sometimes!

> I agree with the larger point that a careful player *may* be able to
> manage the problem, and I've been actively playing that way ever since
> I noticed the effect, but I'm skeptical that extreme carelessness is
> the sole path there. For the moment, after a few initial encounters,
> I'm being careful by getting Liches the hell out of the game.

Many Nethack players are over-cautious, and genociding L certainly
removes a source of potential problems.  However, it still doesn't change that
it takes four casts to destroy a single piece of undamaged armor if the
player has MR.

The player generally wears at least five pieces of armor -- so the
destruction/damage will be significantly spread out, and it'll take a
lot more than four casts.  And this is all assuming the player just ignores
the problem, and never fixes any armor, ever!

If the player doesn't have MR, this spell is a lot nastier, of course.
But Spork is actually _nicer_ than vanilla here, which just destroyed hunks of
your armor without any recourse or warning.

